Service Types (Unit Files)
Services are defined in unit files located in:
   /etc/systemd/system/ - Custom/local services, highest priority, overrides everything else
   /lib/systemd/system/ - Package-provided services, application's documentation or manual located here.
   /usr/lib/systemd/system/ - Distribution default services, Units installed by packages 
The Golden Rule: systemd always checks (/etc/) first. If it doesn't find a rule or instructions there,
it falls back to the default instructions (/usr/lib/).

- Common service unit types:
.service - System services. unit to decide what should run.
.timer - Scheduled tasks (new cron alternatives),  unit to decide when something should run unit to decide what should run.
.timer & .service combine to replace cron
.socket - Network sockets
.target - Group of services (e.g., multi-user.target)

Unit Type     Extension                    Primary Purpose                                      Real-World Example
Service       .service       Manages background processes, daemons, and applications           .nginx.service, sshd.service
Target        .target      Groups multiple units together to define system states/milestones    .multi-user.target, graphical.target
Timer         .timer       Schedules tasks based on time (replaces cron)                        .logrotate.timer
Socket        .socket      Implements socket-based activation for services                       .docker.socket
Mount         .mount       Controls filesystem mount points (replaces or wraps /etc/fstab)       .home.mount   
Automount    .automount    On-demand mounting of filesystems when accessed                  .proc-sys-fs-binfmt_misc.automount
Path        .pathTriggers  a service when files/directories are modified or created                 .cups.path
Slice        .slice        Manages hardware resource allocation (CPU, RAM) using Cgroups         .user.slice, system.slice
Scope       .scope         Tracks foreign, externally-created processes (e.g., user sessions)    .session-1.scope
Swap        .swap          Configures swap space memory management                                .dev-sda2.swap
Device      .device         Exposes udev hardware devices to systemd dependency trees             .dev-ttyS0.device

Service Type= Directives
When creating or modifying a .service file, the Type= key inside the [Service] section dictates how systemd executes the process 
and determines when it has successfully started.
Here is a breakdown of the primary service types:
- Type=simple (Default if omitted)
Behavior: systemd considers the service started immediately as soon as it forks the main process, without waiting to see
if the process succeeds or fails. 
*NOTE: A process forks when it duplicates itself into a parent and a child process. 
*systemd starts the initial process
*That process forks — the child becomes the actual long-running daemon
*The parent process exits, systemd needs to know: "the parent quitting is expected, don't treat it as a crash — track the child instead"
Best Used For: Standard foreground applications that do not fork into background processes.

- Type=exec
Behavior: Similar to simple, but systemd waits until the process binary has been loaded and successfully executed 
before marking the service as active.
Best Used For: Standard modern daemons where startup sequence accuracy matters.

- Type=forking
Behavior: Used for traditional Unix daemons. The initial binary spawns (forks) a child process, and the original parent process exits 
while the child continues running in the background. systemd considers the service ready once the parent process exits.
Requirement: Must usually specify PIDFile= so systemd can track the child process.
Best Used For: Legacy applications that handle their own daemonization (e.g., traditional web servers, MySQL).

- Type=oneshot
Behavior: The process runs, performs a task, and exits completely. systemd waits for the process to terminate before moving on to dependent services.
Best Used For: One-time cleanup scripts, network setup tasks, or initialization steps.
Key Combo: Paired with RemainAfterExit=yes so systemd considers the service "active" even after the process has exited.

-Type=notify
Behavior: Identical to Type=exec, but systemd waits for the application itself to explicitly send a readiness message via sd_notify() before considering it fully started.
Best Used For: Modern, systemd-aware daemons that have a multi-stage startup process (e.g., database loading caches into memory before accepting connections).

- Type=dbus
Behavior: The service is considered ready when it claims a specified name on the system D-Bus message queue.
Requirement: Must specify BusName=.
Best Used For: System services that interact directly with the D-Bus system bus (e.g., NetworkManager, Bluetooth services).

